/dts-v1/;
/*
 * ospi-overlay.dts - wolfBoot PR 1 (QEMU local Linux test) additions on
 * top of QEMU's own amd-versal2-virt DTB (dumpdtb). Applied by mk_dtb.sh
 * with fdtoverlay (fragment format), then one `fdtput` (also in mk_dtb.sh)
 * patches the `clocks` property in place because fdtoverlay renumbers the
 * overlay's phandles and QEMU's base clock nodes carry no labels.
 *
 * Two things only:
 *
 * 1. /chosen: the Linux command line. The console is the PL011 at
 *    0xF1930000 (UART1) - the same UART wolfBoot uses (DEBUG_UART_NUM=1)
 *    and QEMU serial port 2 (the test script captures it). earlycon binds
 *    to the stdout-path device. maxcpus=1: the QEMU machine boots only
 *    CPU0 (secondaries are power-off, no PLM/TF-A PSCI); see the "QEMU
 *    limitations" section of BUILD.md. No root= needed: the FIT ramdisk
 *    is the root filesystem (wolfBoot FIT_RAMDISK=1, /chosen/linux,
 *    initrd-{start,end}).
 *
 * 2. The OSPI flash controller node. QEMU's dump has none (the model has
 *    no FDT support), so it is added here with the shape AMD's VEK385
 *    DTB uses for the same controller (evidence/vek385-prebuilt/
 *    dtb-extract.txt, /axi/spi@f1010000). Run 11 compatible decision:
 *
 *      compatible = "cdns,qspi-nor" "qemu,xlnx-versal-ospi-nor";
 *
 *    i.e. the board's first string "amd,versal2-ospi" is DROPPED for the
 *    QEMU build (it stays in the board DTB). The stock driver
 *    (drivers/spi/spi-cadence-quadspi.c) selects its platform quirks by
 *    compatible (cqspi_dt_ids[]); "amd,versal2-ospi" -> versal2_ospi
 *    platdata = CQSPI_SUPPORTS_OCTAL + CQSPI_SUPPORT_EXTERNAL_DMA, which
 *    in the stock driver means:
 *      (a) aligned bulk reads go through the CSU DMA only after a
 *          zynqmp_pm_ospi_mux_select() PM ioctl (IOCTL_OSPI_MUX_SELECT =
 *          21) over the SMC conduit; in QEMU the zynqmp firmware driver
 *          never completes its PM SMC handshake (zero "SMC 0x..." trace
 *          lines in the console in any Run 11 boot, no "Platform
 *          Management API v1.0" probe message, and the first (SFDP) DMA
 *          read fails with "spi-nor spi0.0: CS0: operation failed with
 *          -19" = -ENODEV from zynqmp_pm_ioctl with no firmware ops);
 *      (b) the OCTAL hwcaps make spi-nor switch the flash to 8D-8D-8D
 *          (DTR/DDR, DQS-based). The QEMU OSPI model has no DQS/DDR PHY
 *          fidelity, so the driver's periodic delay-line tuning
 *          (CQSPI_TUNING_PERIODICITY_MS = 300000 ms; cqspi_setdlldelay:
 *          tap-scan + JEDEC-ID match window >= 3) fails with -EINVAL
 *          ("CS0: periodic tuning failed (-22)") about 5 min into the
 *          wbmtd update write and the write aborts (the Run 11 L2
 *          blocker).
 *    With the generic "cdns,qspi-nor" string the driver binds the
 *    cdns_qspi platdata (quirks = CQSPI_DISABLE_DAC_MODE only):
 *    no EXTERNAL_DMA (no firmware/mux-select dependency; reads use the
 *    APB indirect-read engine + DAC data window - the same path
 *    wolfBoot uses, proven by the FIT load), no OCTAL hwcaps (the flash
 *    stays SDR; the periodic tuning is then an SDR tap-scan, whose
 *    JEDEC-ID reads the model answers correctly), STIG enabled,
 *    wr-completion on. Everything else (register layout, indirect write
 *    + completion IRQ, fixed partitions) is identical, and on real
 *    VEK385 hardware the board DTB keeps "amd,versal2-ospi" (the stock
 *    versal2_ospi path is the board-correct one; the string change is
 *    QEMU-only, which is why the DTB overlay - not the driver - carries
 *    it). The trailing "qemu,xlnx-versal-ospi-nor" string is inert now
 *    that the QEMU-only kdrv module (tools/scripts/versal2/qemu/linux/
 *    kdrv/versal2-ospi-nor.c) was removed in Run 11 - the stock
 *    "cdns,qspi-nor" driver works in QEMU - but it is kept in the
 *    compatible list so the DTB layout stays stable.
 *
 *    "cdns,qspi-nor" is the Linux compatible for the Cadence QSPI/OSPI
 *    controller; the flash child "jedec,spi-nor" is picked up by the
 *    standard SPI-NOR MTD driver, so NO custom kernel or driver module
 *    is needed: the stock AMD EDF kernel probes QEMU's OSPI model
 *    (hw/ssi/xlnx-versal-ospi.c: APB registers at 0xF1010000, DAC
 *    window at 0xC0000000 size 0x20000000, GIC SPI 216 on this machine,
 *    CSU DMA at 0xF1011000/0xF1011800 - all modelled). Note the IRQ
 *    number is machine-dependent: amd-versal2-virt uses VERSAL2_MAP in
 *    hw/arm/xlnx-versal.c, whose .ospi.irq is 216 (the .ospi.irq = 124
 *    entry belongs to the Versal Gen1 VERSAL_MAP; the VEK385 board DTB
 *    also says 124, but QEMU's machine wires 216 - see the
 *    interrupts property below for the full evidence).
 *
 *    The stock driver's cqspi_of_get_pdata() (EDF 6.18.10-xilinx)
 *    REQUIRES cdns,fifo-depth / cdns,fifo-width / cdns,trigger-address
 *    on the controller and cdns,read-delay / cdns,tshsl-ns /
 *    cdns,tsd2d-ns / cdns,tchsh-ns / cdns,tslch-ns on the flash node
 *    (missing cdns,read-delay is the run-9 "couldn't determine
 *    read-delay" + "failed to setup flash parameters -6"). The system
 *    dtc 1.7.2 cannot parse comma-containing property names, so all of
 *    them are written by mk_dtb.sh into the combined tree
 *    (fdt_setprop.py --cells) with the exact VEK385 values
 *    (fifo-depth 256 = the model's detected depth, no
 *    "FIFO depth (256) different from config" warning; trigger-address
 *    0xc0000000; read-delay 0, tshsl-ns 0, tsd2d-ns 0, tchsh-ns 1,
 *    tslch-ns 1).
 *
 *    The /firmware node below ("xlnx,versal2-firmware", method "smc") is
 *    kept for the zynqmp firmware driver. With the cdns_qspi platdata
 *    (see above) the OSPI driver no longer depends on it; the trampoline
 *    still implements the PM SMC answers (see trampoline.S, AMD System
 *    Controller PM API section).
 *
 *    The fixed-partitions children must match
 *    config/examples/versal2_vek385_linux.config exactly (run-13 layout;
 *    old run 3-12 values BOOT 0x19C0000 / UPDATE 0x4600000 kept for
 *    reference - run 13 layout decision: 8 MB BOOT.BIN reservation, AMD
 *    BOOT.BIN 5.31 MiB, evidence/mdom-bank-analysis-run9.txt):
 *      BOOT   0x1DC0000  44 MB   (/dev/mtd* "boot")
 *      UPDATE 0x49C0000  44 MB   (/dev/mtd* "update")
 *      SWAP   0xFA40000  64 KB   (/dev/mtd* "swap")
 *
 *    The flash@0 reg is the chip-select (0) per the controller's
 *    #address-cells=1 / #size-cells=0. The flash timing properties match
 *    the VEK385 DTB at its 20 MHz spi-max-frequency (the QEMU model has
 *    no PHY timing, so the driver's delay-line tuning writes are
 *    register-only there).
 */
/ {
	fragment@0 {
		target-path = "/chosen";
		__overlay__ {
			/* The bootargs are the same shape AMD's own VEK385 DTB
			 * uses (evidence/vek385-prebuilt/dtb-extract.txt, /chosen
			 * "earlycon=pl011,mmio32,0xf1930000 console=ttyAMA..."),
			 * adapted to QEMU's machine:
			 *   earlycon=pl011,mmio32,0xf1930000 - the PL011 earlycon
			 *     driver prints from the earliest point (the EDF kernel
			 *     carries it; its __earlycon_table entry "pl011" is in
			 *     the Image). With the explicit device it does not
			 *     depend on stdout-path.
			 *   console=ttyAMA0,115200 - QEMU's base DTB lists
			 *     /uart@f1930000 (UART1) before /uart@f1920000, so the
			 *     PL011 at 0xF1930000 (the console port, QEMU serial1)
			 *     is ttyAMA0 here (the VEK385 board DTB has it as
			 *     ttyAMA1 because its UART0 node comes first).
			 *   maxcpus=1 - the QEMU machine boots only CPU0 (secondary
			 *     cores are power-off, no PLM/TF-A PSCI).
			 *   rdinit=/init - the initramfs /init (busybox shell +
			 *     wbmtd) is the root; no root= argument is needed.
			 * stdout-path stays the console UART so any
			 * stdout-path-based (parameter-less) earlycon also binds to
			 * 0xF1930000. */
			/*
			 * NOTE on console tty numbering (verified from the kernel's own
			 * probe log on this QEMU): the PL011 driver assigns
			 *   ttyAMA0 = 0xf1920000 (UART0, QEMU serial0 -> "-serial null")
			 *   ttyAMA1 = 0xf1930000 (UART1, QEMU serial1 -> the capture)
			 * (the tty numbers do NOT follow the DTB node order). The console
			 * must therefore be ttyAMA1 (0xF1930000) - the same UART wolfBoot
			 * uses (DEBUG_UART_NUM=1) and the one QEMU serial1 captures. Using
			 * ttyAMA0 sends the kernel's final console to serial0 (null) and
			 * all post-boot output is lost. */
			stdout-path = "/uart@f1930000";
			bootargs = "earlycon=pl011,mmio32,0xf1930000 console=ttyAMA1,115200 maxcpus=1 rdinit=/init initcall_debug";
		};
	};

	fragment@1 {
		target-path = "/";
		__overlay__ {
			/* OSPI NOR flash controller (Cadence, QEMU xlnx-versal-ospi
			 * model). The root node uses #address-cells/#size-cells = 2, so
			 * the reg values are 64-bit (high,low) pairs. Two regions, same
			 * order as AMD's VEK385 DTB:
			 *   reg 0: APB register file at 0xF1010000 (model: 0x104 of
			 *          registers; the 64 KB window matches the board DTB).
			 *   reg 1: AXI DAC window (512 MB) at 0xC0000000; the 256 MB
			 *          flash maps to the lower half (offset == flash addr). */
			qspi@f1010000 {
				/* The final property is the two-string
				 * compatible = "amd,versal2-ospi" "cdns,qspi-nor";
				 * but the system dtc 1.7.2 (Debian) cannot parse multi-string
				 * property values in DTS source at all ("model = \"a\" \"b\";"
				 * is a syntax error), so the node is compiled with the single
				 * "cdns,qspi-nor" string and mk_dtb.sh rewrites the property
				 * in the binary tree with libfdt (fdt_setprop.py). */
				compatible = "cdns,qspi-nor";
				reg = <0x0 0xf1010000 0x0 0x10000
				       0x0 0xc0000000 0x0 0x20000000>;
				/* GIC SPI 216 (QEMU amd-versal2-virt machine wiring).
				 * The stock driver's indirect ops complete on an IRQ:
				 * cqspi_indirect_write_execute() arms IRQMASK and waits
				 * for the completion IRQ; the CSU DMA read path completes
				 * on the DMA-done IRQ. The QEMU machine ORs the OSPI IRQ
				 * and both CSU DMA IRQs onto ONE GIC SPI line
				 * (versal_create_ospi(), hw/arm/xlnx-versal.c:
				 * create_or_gate(..., 3, map->irq)); for amd-versal2-virt
				 * (VERSAL_VER_VERSAL2 -> VERSAL2_MAP) that line is
				 * map->ospi.irq = 216. The old value below (124) matched
				 * the VEK385 board DTB and the VERSAL_MAP (Gen1) entry, but
				 * not this machine: with 124 the kernel armed the wrong GIC
				 * line, every indirect write timed out ("Indirect write
				 * timeout", probe failed with -110, no /dev/mtd) even
				 * though the model completed the ops (QEMU model trace,
				 * Run 11). 216 is unused by every other node of QEMU's
				 * base DTB and by every other device of VERSAL2_MAP.
				 * Old (wrong for this machine) value, kept for reference:
				 *   interrupts = <0x0 124 0x4>;  // board/Gen1 value */
				interrupts = <0x0 216 0x4>;
				clock-names = "ref";
				/* The stock driver (cqspi_of_get_pdata) REQUIRES
				 * cdns,fifo-width and cdns,trigger-address and uses
				 * cdns,fifo-depth to size its indirect read chunks;
				 * the system dtc 1.7.2 cannot parse property names
				 * containing commas, so mk_dtb.sh writes all three as
				 * 32-bit cells into the combined tree (fdt_setprop.py
				 * --cells): fifo-width=4, trigger-address=0xc0000000
				 * (the DAC window base), fifo-depth=4 (chunk = 12 bytes,
				 * inside the model's indac trigger range). */
				#address-cells = <1>;
				#size-cells = <0>;

				/* The NOR flash on chip-select 0. The timing
				 * properties (cdns,read-delay, cdns,tshsl-ns,
				 * cdns,tsd2d-ns, cdns,tchsh-ns,
				 * cdns,tslch-ns) are REQUIRED by the stock
				 * driver (cqspi_of_get_flash_pdata; their
				 * absence is the run-9 "couldn't determine
				 * read-delay" / -ENODEV probe failure) but the
				 * system dtc 1.7.2 cannot parse the
				 * comma-containing names, so mk_dtb.sh writes
				 * them with the VEK385 values (read-delay 0,
				 * tshsl-ns 0, tsd2d-ns 0, tchsh-ns 1,
				 * tslch-ns 1). */
				flash@0 {
					compatible = "jedec,spi-nor";
					reg = <0>;
					/* 20 MHz, the VEK385 value (QEMU's "ref"
					 * clock is the 125 MHz /clk125 fixed
					 * clock; the driver derives its sclk
					 * divisor from it). */
					spi-max-frequency = <20000000>;
					/* SDR only (Run 11): the generic cdns_qspi platdata
					 * advertises no octal/quad hwcaps, so spi-nor keeps
					 * the flash in standard SPI mode; the 8-IO values
					 * below would push it to 8D-8D-8D and the QEMU
					 * model's periodic DQS/DDR tuning cannot follow.
					 * Kept for reference (the VEK385 board values):
					 *   spi-rx-bus-width = <8>;
					 *   spi-tx-bus-width = <8>;
					 */
					#address-cells = <1>;
					#size-cells = <1>;

					partitions {
						compatible = "fixed-partitions";
						#address-cells = <1>;
						#size-cells = <1>;

						/* wolfBoot BOOT partition (config:
						 * WOLFBOOT_PARTITION_BOOT_ADDRESS=0x1DC0000,
						 * WOLFBOOT_PARTITION_SIZE=0x2C00000; old run 3-12
						 * node: partition@19c0000, reg <0x19c0000 0x2c00000>) */
						partition@1dc0000 {
							reg = <0x1dc0000 0x2c00000>;
							label = "boot";
						};

						/* wolfBoot UPDATE partition (config:
						 * WOLFBOOT_PARTITION_UPDATE_ADDRESS=0x49C0000; old
						 * run 3-12 node: partition@4600000,
						 * reg <0x4600000 0x2c00000>) */
						partition@49c0000 {
							reg = <0x49c0000 0x2c00000>;
							label = "update";
						};

						/* wolfBoot SWAP / trailer staging sector
						 * (config: WOLFBOOT_PARTITION_SWAP_ADDRESS=
						 * 0xFA40000, WOLFBOOT_SECTOR_SIZE=0x10000) */
						partition@fa40000 {
							reg = <0xfa40000 0x10000>;
							label = "swap";
						};
					};
				};
			};
		};
	};

	/*
	 * The zynqmp System-Controller firmware node. The stock
	 * spi-cadence-quadspi driver's versal2_ospi data selects the DMA
	 * path (CQSPI_SUPPORTS_OCTAL -> use_dma_read); every DMA read first
	 * issues zynqmp_pm_ospi_mux_select() (PM_IOCTL /
	 * IOCTL_OSPI_MUX_SELECT) through the zynqmp firmware driver
	 * (drivers/firmware/xilinx/zynqmp-pm.c), which needs this DTB node
	 * to probe at all - otherwise the mux select fails and every DMA
	 * read returns -ENODEV. The kernel requires the method property
	 * ("smc" or "hvc"); "smc" because QEMU's trampoline answers the
	 * PM SMCs at EL3 (see trampoline.S, AMD System Controller PM API
	 * section). The VEK385 DTB carries the same node (with a
	 * power-domains cell the driver parses as its pd_dev_id; QEMU has
	 * no System Controller power domain, and the trampoline no-ops the
	 * mux select regardless of the id, so it is omitted here).
	 */
	fragment@2 {
		target-path = "/";
		__overlay__ {
			firmware {
				compatible = "xlnx,versal2-firmware";
				method = "smc";
				status = "okay";
			};
		};
	};
};
